  <h1>Who I Am</h1>

  <p>
    Greetings, my name is Mark Joshua Eslao, you can call me MJ. I’ve always been drawn to understanding things more deeply — not just what happened, but why it happened, what shaped it, and what it means after. I've always had the belief that everything happens for a reason, and for every reason, there is a connection and a story to be told. I’m fascinated by the way things are built, how they work, and how they fail. I’m also fascinated by the people who build them, the people who use them, and the people who are affected by them. I like to think about the systems that shape our lives, the patterns that emerge from them, and the spaces in between where things can be different.
  </p>

  <p>
    A lot of my life has been spent around tools, systems, and technology, but I don’t see them as separate from people. The way something is built, the way it fails, and the way it is experienced all say something about the world behind it. We all don't build systems only, but rather we see it as a project, a process, and a practice. Starting from scratch and an initial plan, we continue to build, iterate, and reflect on the work we do. Every step we take in the development of the systems we make, we all continue to learn, expanding our horizons and deepening our understanding, not just systems we build, but also the world we live in and the people we share it with.
  </p>

  <p>
    I value clarity, sincerity, and thoughtfulness. I like work that has structure, but I also like space for feeling, memory, and reflection. While we do best in all of our works, I think it is also best that we value the process of making, learning, and sharing. We all have our own way of doing things, and I think it’s important to find a way that works for you, that allows you to express yourself, and that allows you to connect with others. I also think it’s important to be open to new ideas, new perspectives, and new ways of doing things. We all have a lot to learn from each other, and I think it’s important to be open to that learning, to be open to the world around us, and to be open to the people we share it with.
  </p>

  <p>
    This portfolio is not just about what I do. It’s also about how I think, what I notice, and the kind of person I’m still becoming.
  </p>
</div>